  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education Initiative to minimize human impact on California condor populations in Mexico</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The California condor is a highly endangered species in need of active management in order to survive. Since 2003, several of the birds have been released and in 2013, three chicks were successfully hatched and fledged for the first time in Mexico. These achievements need to be accompanied by public awareness, education, and outreach campaigns in order to bring the California Condor back from the brink of extinction. Through this project, the San Diego Zoo Institute for Conservation Research will: 1) work to increase the local and national awareness about the threats affecting the survival of the California condor; 2) increase the San Pedro Martir National Park capacity as a venue for conservation education and outreach; and 3) increase the capacity of Chapultepec Zoo educators, park rangers and local school teachers and museum employees to implement environmental education and outreach strategies.</narrative>
  </description>
